{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1486760-large-scale-fights","id":1486760,"title":"Large scale fights","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-07-17T05:28:15-07:00","id":1486760,"title":"Large scale fights","content":"I'd like to trigger a discussion on your way to handle large scale fights. There may be several different situation which I'm listing here below:<br /> <br /> - Multitude of &quot;normal&quot; people against one character: One by one they may be not an issue for a Vampire but when 10 or more of them are focusing on a single character they may become a very hard threat to handle. By book each of them (up to 4 per turn) will do their attack with the defense of the character being lowered by one after any consecutive attack and the vampire will be only able to hit to one per time. This for sure a way to handle but can also be very heavy in terms of rolls per turn. is there any simplified approach which, non the less, gives a good feeling of the danger? Perhaps a teamwork could apply?<br /> <br /> - Many NPC vs Many NPC (where vampire may or may not be involved along one side): here the example of two gangs openly fighting, two small army/hunters group against character allies etc. Here there's really no need to roll one by one. I was thinking to something like handling the two groups as a unique entity but how to handle the size (which my be different) or specific &quot;quality&quot; of the two parties? Perhaps one side may be a larger amount of enemies but the other side is being composed by ghouls or trained people. And how to handle the active involvement of PCs? <br /> <br /> <br /> Fall of Camarilla provide a quite nice example for Riots which I would only use for an all vs all situation, not for a focused fight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-17T07:21:41-07:00","id":1486766,"title":"","content":"Deviant: the Renegades has rules for Goons to model large numbers of mechanically identical opposing characters as a collective opponent in a sidebar on Page 200.<br /> <br /> A group of goons all acts on the same Initiative, adds dice to its rolls based on the number of additional actors (+2 per extra goon for actions within their role, +1 per extra goon for slightly out of the ordinary actions, +1 per two extra goons rounded up for actions well outside their skillset), and effectively has a collective Health rating (technically just the number of goons, a la Swarms and their radius) wherein the Beaten Down threshold (based on Stamina) determines if a successful attack takes a single goon out of the fight (goons immune to the Beaten Down Tilt for whatever reason instead lose a goon for every two successful attacks that deal enough damage, and area attacks and Tilts can take out multiple goons and/or shortcut past the damage threshold at ST discretion). You can split up and merge groups of goons at the beginning of their turn as they take the same or different actions, such as to attack two different characters or maintain a grapple and pursue another target.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-17T09:10:02-07:00","id":1486775,"title":"","content":"There have been <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1157673-simplifying-mass-combat-representing-a-crowd-of-npcs-as-a-single-ch\">discussions about mass combat in the CoD</a></b> before on this forum. Could it be a suggestion? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27293,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27293-cabaal","user":"Cabaal","date":". 07-17-2022, 12:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27293-cabaal","name":"Cabaal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27293_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-17T11:13:33-07:00","id":1486780,"title":"","content":"The first example works with Goons, as mentioned before, or a Horde Horror from the CofD core book. Just don't give them a Dread Power or give them ones that fits the group and can be justified in a mundane way.<br /> <br /> The second, I tend to run by just having the PCs only fight a subset of the big group and having the fight go about as well for the NPCs as it did for the PCs. If you must roll, can probably just roll a pool based on each group's abilities (much like how CofD does NPCs in general, with pools for general competence). If they are a major focus of the game and you really want them statted out, nabbing the Attributes and Actions from Mummy Cults/Deviant Conspiracies would probably work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-17T23:19:35-07:00","id":1486845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1486760-large-scale-fights#post1486760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Many NPC vs Many NPC (where vampire may or may not be involved along one side): here the example of two gangs openly fighting, two small army/hunters group against character allies etc. Here there's really no need to roll one by one. I was thinking to something like handling the two groups as a unique entity but how to handle the size (which my be different) or specific &quot;quality&quot; of the two parties? Perhaps one side may be a larger amount of enemies but the other side is being composed by ghouls or trained people. And how to handle the active involvement of PCs?</div> </div> </div> </div>When the advantages of both sides are so different that no easy trait comparison is possible, the simple solution is for the ST to decide on a relative dice pool for each side that encompasses each sides unique inherent and situational advantages. I. e. if the ST believes that one side is significantly more like to win than the other, but doesn't think it is an entirely foregone conclusion, they could roll 10 dice vs. 5 dice. If something slightly more mechanically robust is needed, something which could be rolled over multiple turns, treat each side like a single NPC with regular traits. Both sides have distinct attack dice pools, Defense, Armor, Willpower reserves, other fuel reserves (if supernatural) and then you just have these two hack at each other as if they were just two dudes. Things like Damage ratings could represent both the majority armament in a group but also any situational advantage that would make them more hurty.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-30T21:55:00-07:00","id":1495943,"title":"","content":"The only time I've used large scale-fights was in Tudor England with a group of angry-peasants v. a vampire, and I just rolled out the combat for the two-score participants; it didn't go well for the neonate. But he wanted to stand his ground.<br /> <br /> As I recalled, I think Vampire: Dark Ages Storytellers Companion has a mass-combat system in which a character just rolled for damage and tried not to slip and think there was another were whole groups (Armies), would be destroyed or route; both, or maybe they were the same system, were very abstract.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-04T06:42:53-07:00","id":1496190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1486760-large-scale-fights#post1486760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like to trigger a discussion on your way to handle large scale fights. There may be several different situation which I'm listing here below:<br /> <br /> - Multitude of &quot;normal&quot; people against one character: One by one they may be not an issue for a Vampire but when 10 or more of them are focusing on a single character they may become a very hard threat to handle. By book each of them (up to 4 per turn) will do their attack with the defense of the character being lowered by one after any consecutive attack and the vampire will be only able to hit to one per time. This for sure a way to handle but can also be very heavy in terms of rolls per turn. is there any simplified approach which, non the less, gives a good feeling of the danger? Perhaps a teamwork could apply?<br /> <br /> - Many NPC vs Many NPC (where vampire may or may not be involved along one side): here the example of two gangs openly fighting, two small army/hunters group against character allies etc. Here there's really no need to roll one by one. I was thinking to something like handling the two groups as a unique entity but how to handle the size (which my be different) or specific &quot;quality&quot; of the two parties? Perhaps one side may be a larger amount of enemies but the other side is being composed by ghouls or trained people. And how to handle the active involvement of PCs?<br /> <br /> <br /> Fall of Camarilla provide a quite nice example for Riots which I would only use for an all vs all situation, not for a focused fight.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> For the first situation I'd go with the Goon rules introduced in Deviant as already stated by Satchel.<br /> <br /> For the second one I'd use the rules in Geist for Krewe VS. Krewe conflicts, (Krewes are literal groups of people lead by sin-eaters, that's why they aplly well on the second situation, because the krewe is a large party of people acting as one) though that requires you to give a few stats to the group (Power, Finesse, Resistance, Merits, Esotery and Doctrines).<br /> <br /> The rules for conflicts between Krewes however are written for long-term conflict with each clash of Krewes generating damage for their respective health bars (called Congregation) and that might not be what you have in mind if you want the conflict to be over at the end os a scene with a clear victorious party. In that case, I'd use the sidebar rules &quot;Bringing a Krewe to a gun fight&quot; where it states that the PC character and the NPC character engage in a Down and Dirty conflict with each side rolling as normal with a bonus equal to their respective Krewe Power stat. The victorious side defeats the other in a single roll and that is it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25516-pinkhaunt","name":"PinkHaunt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25516_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}